/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106769 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4e3d714f-5ee5-588e-a541-2b56e8ec5909" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106769 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105825 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106770 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106776 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106770 #edb/ref 1061 27401 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106770 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2e6311d2-17da-5bd4-a8bb-025d853635b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106770 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106771 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106772 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106771 #edb/ref 1061 27402 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106771 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cf257099-cebd-5c9d-9671-6b14b1f63d0d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106771 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186107105 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106772 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106769 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106772 #edb/ref 1061 27403 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106772 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "23b77bef-14a9-5128-9f05-05b523b37275" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106772 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106776 #edb/ref 1061 27600 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106776 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "27015fb3-f631-50ed-9981-c2dd89b9bb19" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106776 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105830 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107090 #edb/ref 1064 "Given a matrix $A,$ an **eigenvector** of $A$ is a *non-zero* vector $\\mathbf{v}$ such that \n\n$$\nA \\mathbf{v} = \\lambda \\mathbf{v}\n$$\n\nfor some scalar $\\lambda \\in \\mathbb{R}.$ The scalar $\\lambda$ is called the **eigenvalue** corresponding to the eigenvector $\\mathbf{v}.$\n\nFor example, consider the matrix \n![](images/63/63e2c65a23a0680412a14084d543b6cc19c0529fd80863b09c99987d309eaa4f.svg) \nand the vector ![](images/b7/b7811b74d8d3075357925864b5be27f57b292dadca9c04d222954baa1e66ba2a.svg) Computing the product $A \\cdot \\mathbf{v},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe've discovered that $A {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}} = {\\color{red}-2} {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}}.$ Therefore,\n\n- the vector ![](images/13/13a28e543647ede7a88b30fb652bd7fba6c9adb8ee3142161a102de947ef2d2a.svg) is an *eigenvector* of $A,$ and\n\n- the scalar $\\color{red}\\lambda=-2$ is the *eigenvalue* corresponding to the eigenvector ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}}.$\n\n**Caution:** Remember that an eigenvector must be a *non-zero* vector. This is very important." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107090 #edb/ref 1065 3557 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107090 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1014a8e8-38ce-568b-86d7-22f32ef8f697" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107090 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107098 #edb/ref 1064 "Eigenvectors and eigenvalues can be interpreted geometrically.\n\nFirst, recall that if the matrix ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) is the standard matrix of some linear transformation ![](images/80/80d488fc9d8168cecdf882f1aa178325bb36bd387dd9f86f2b0cd98a5c54f4dc.svg) then the product ![](images/dc/dcc52971e9a7e86ebbe8bf972c562fce13d389f9261b69cba05dcabb1426b9dc.svg) gives the image of ![](images/4f/4fa2300af121b336bb0d7845b8e2b3e34be5a517a1bca45b4ec7b6514b2c544d.svg) under the action of ![](images/a1/a1af41bdb2f2fe410c6f8e02d1a2a0048f0aa470ceef4508753090444ca039be.svg) The image of ![](images/4f/4fa2300af121b336bb0d7845b8e2b3e34be5a517a1bca45b4ec7b6514b2c544d.svg) under the action of ![](images/c1/c15eeb9e7a3ff525fbdcd2f175622bd034f0b34899224f9ce2e08a5300d625de.svg) can appear quite different from the original vector ![](images/92/9221d4dc55c1df8da019956ea23f9d4f9ff3f5b04921ec2158804a31a50e636b.svg)\n\nHowever, for an eigenvector ![](images/77/771507e21f2935d77160257f7d56c3f5fcef1d0a114ac8a38c373c49790dd854.svg) with corresponding eigenvalue ![](images/4a/4a2f3891f0b4c8b852767d556e1607028035219b70ab83b4910e95159a930b9c.svg) we get\n\n![](images/ee/eed93a562368727b490c2b63d14f46580dcd0439ac90df23a23f75a31de9e971.svg)\n\nIn other words, when the matrix ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) is applied to an eigenvector ![](images/b2/b2766af4a0fe79e81fbd5ac113f0446ce1d111def401abbf09ced1ee62ca1cf1.svg) the image of ![](images/77/771507e21f2935d77160257f7d56c3f5fcef1d0a114ac8a38c373c49790dd854.svg) is a scaled version of the original vector ![](images/77/771507e21f2935d77160257f7d56c3f5fcef1d0a114ac8a38c373c49790dd854.svg), and the scale factor is ![](images/44/44db815dcaf0a9d405026460a655495e055df7e55a785f542f8920ca90af5418.svg)\n\nTo demonstrate, consider once again the following matrix:\n\n![](images/43/43e76f5a5e889ec18a6893be8cdbf4837d3dedfab149a0981ce6619a62718170.svg)\n\nWe've already seen that ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) has an eigenvector ![](images/fe/fef256ade4f52003da67fac5af68ff4b0d4e7f04ef55e72bb0279bdc8f1e8c3c.svg) with corresponding eigenvalue ![](images/b5/b5cdbd2f286044241daca2565a1ab7f70196ecc2cf6503965d9a820faad9b06e.svg) So we have\n\n![](images/2d/2da94bfdde0e3650ded2622748ead5a4c09cabf610f9d181d48ae158d9bd0986.svg)\n\nPlotting the vector ![](images/77/771507e21f2935d77160257f7d56c3f5fcef1d0a114ac8a38c373c49790dd854.svg) and its image ![](images/ab/abe8027351c1645f4c582440b8b536f48f3ec35d008570f3432f322fa468e861.svg) in the plane, we get the following picture.\n\n![](images/9a/9aca41e0597b0b1698e8ec24da9966fd9b7e5fee3951081d8abdb0cc0caaac7b.png)\n\nIn general, when we apply a matrix ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) to an eigenvector ![](images/b2/b2766af4a0fe79e81fbd5ac113f0446ce1d111def401abbf09ced1ee62ca1cf1.svg) the resulting vector ![](images/be/befecb9d885ee3bbbb7313401ca3671428e4aced68a4bfdb4b2fc9a005ea2b43.svg) always lies on the same line as ![](images/1f/1f3d1a60926323e963d7563529669e5cf8210771c8cbb0379f7a7d90e7c6aeec.svg)\n\nIf ![](images/8c/8c453358dacec49655b560be52a99066e3cab1d34cfbdbdbaa7bd74d868fef82.svg) then the matrix stretches the vector. For ![](images/ea/ea6d742051f82ff2f258e08ed09c3b3dc6d4c7f9bbbe40fea718aec8183dc44f.svg) the matrix compresses the vector. In addition, a negative eigenvalue ![](images/c9/c91cda6703c5767aaafeca807b07e466f160cb0ade84472ade5fa85d71a5713e.svg) means that ![](images/77/771507e21f2935d77160257f7d56c3f5fcef1d0a114ac8a38c373c49790dd854.svg) is flipped in the opposite direction as well as scaled." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107098 #edb/ref 1065 3963 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107098 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "cdf6ca75-e37b-552a-a106-ee8ac2f36a61" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107098 #edb/ref 1067 "The Geometric Interpretation of Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107105 #edb/ref 1064 "A **binomial distribution** is a probability distribution that models the number of successes in a sequence of $n$ independent experiments (or trials) where each outcome is either success or failure. In a binomial distribution, the probability of success in each experiment is the same.\n\nIf there are $n$ experiments and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment, then the number of successes has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\binom{n}{x} p^x (1-p)^{n-x}, \\qquad x=0,1,2, \\ldots, n\n$$\n\nFor example, if we flip a coin $5$ times, then the number of heads follows a binomial distribution.\n\n- Each toss is an experiment that either results in success (i.e., heads) or failure (i.e., tails).\n\n- There are $n=5$ experiments.\n\n- Each experiment has a probability $p=\\dfrac{1}{2}$ of success.\n\nSo, the number of heads has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x}){(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}{(1-\\frac{1}{2})}^{5-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x}){(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{5-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x}){(\\frac{1}{2})}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen a random variable $X$ follows a binomial distribution consisting of $n$ experiments (or trials) and the probability of success on each trial is $p,$ we often write\n\n$$\nX\\sim B(n,p).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn words, we say, \"the random variable $X$ follows a binomial distribution with $n$ trials and probability $p$ of success on each trial.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107105 #edb/ref 1065 4451 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107105 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "7e7bad04-ccb9-574b-9792-eb4b8a51b62b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107105 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119801 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049537 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119801 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120371 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119801 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120375 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119801 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342108 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119801 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342109 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119801 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186391776 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119801 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186391783 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119801 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186411265 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119801 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186411272 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119801 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating a Multiple of a Vector" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119801 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186119612 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119801 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "1ce19559-0f08-5ef7-9ce4-58e8e14094ab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119802 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120307 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119802 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120374 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1759